Q: Remove const or get a non-const object A: <p>Do <strong>not</strong> remove (cast-away) <code>const</code> from any <code>const</code> object.<br />
At best it will make code difficult to read and maintain because it violates common sense and practices (<em>&quot;Oh the value can't change there, it's a <code>const</code> object</em>&quot;).<br />
At worst it will create terrible, terrible bugs because the compiler assumes that the object cannot change, may optimise using the assumption, which may or may not lead to unexpected behaviour if it does change.</p>
<p>For example:</p>
<pre><code>// A.h
class A
{
  public:
    //changes the value of number anyway by casting away const
    static void doStuff(const int&amp; number); 
}

// B.cpp
bool B::isPositive()
{
    const int i = 2;
    A::doStuff(i);
    return (i &gt; 0);
}
</code></pre>
<p>The compiler may assume that the <code>const i</code> in <code>B::isPositive()</code> does not get changed and thus optimise the <code>return (i &gt; 0);</code> to <code>return true</code> (2 is always greater than 0) - it does not know that ´A::doStuff()´ does actually changes the value.</p>
<p>So, I <strong>strongly</strong> urge you to use a different approach here, e.g. getting a non-const object, as you said.</p>

Q: How to test PlateJs Marks with React Testing Library A: <p>The root cause is that <code>toggleMark</code> immediately returns when <code>editor.selection</code> is <code>null</code> , see <a href="https://github.com/udecode/plate/blob/3add964236e300f93a0783f54395097e6bd28cb3/packages/slate/src/internal/transforms-extension/toggleMark.ts#L11-L12" rel="nofollow noreferrer">implementation</a></p>
<p>In JSDOM (what React Testing Library uses), ContentEditable selection behaviour isn't fully implemented, so clicking the toolbar button fires <code>onClick</code> but the editor has no valid selection — the mark command becomes a no-op, no state changes, no re-render.</p>
<p>to test through click interactions, you need to manually set a valid selection on the editor before clicking the button:</p>
<pre><code>// After render, set selection programmatically
act(() =&gt; {
  editor.tf.select({
    anchor: { path: [0, 0], offset: 0 },
    focus: { path: [0, 0], offset: 5 },
  });
});

await userEvent.click(button);
</code></pre>
